# Modular Architecture Rules

This project uses a highly modular architecture to keep file sizes small (under 400 lines), save AI context limits, and avoid merge conflicts.

## 1. Managing Large Templates (Orchestrator Pattern)
- If a user asks you to add a new feature, modal, or widget to a major page (e.g., `dashboard.html`), **DO NOT** add the code directly into the main orchestrator template unless you are adding a new `{% include %}` tag.
- Instead, locate the appropriate partial file inside its subdirectory (e.g., `billing/templates/billing/dashboard/_modal_recent_logins.html`) and edit that, OR create a new partial file and include it.
- **Never allow a single HTML file to exceed 500 lines.**

## 2. Python Backend Modularity
- If a Python file (`views.py` or `models.py`) exceeds 400 lines, extract logic into smaller module files or a `services.py` layer.

## 3. General Rule
Before writing code, analyze if your change will make a file unnecessarily long. If yes, break it into a separate partial, component, or module first.
